// F-Droid, bundled as a prebuilt so the device has a way to install software
// without a Play Store.
//
// PROVENANCE. Downloaded from https://f-droid.org/F-Droid.apk and verified
// before being committed, because this ends up signed into a system image
// where nobody will look at it again:
//
//   version      1.23.2 (versionCode 1023052), package org.fdroid.fdroid
//   sha256       985f5181d48bb6bafd54083a048b391271e0ab28385881cc41294fb01a222762
//   signing cert 43238d512c1e5eb2d6569f4a3afbf5523418b82e0a3ed1552770abb9a9c9ccab
//                CN=Ciaran Gultnieks, valid 2010-07-23 to 2037-12-08
//
// That fingerprint is F-Droid's published signing key. It is the thing worth
// checking: the download is plain HTTPS, and a matching certificate is what
// makes this the real client rather than merely a file that came from the
// right host. Re-verify it if this APK is ever updated.
//
// presigned rather than re-signed with the platform key. F-Droid must keep its
// own signature: it self-updates from the F-Droid repository, and Android only
// accepts an update signed by the same certificate as the installed copy.
// Re-signing here would install a build that could never update itself.
//
// preprocessed, and the build refuses to proceed without it:
//
//   Prebuilt, presigned apks with targetSdkVersion >= 30 must set
//   preprocessed: true (because they must be signed with signature v2, and the
//   build system would wreck that signature otherwise)
//
// F-Droid targets SDK 30, so it is signed with APK Signature Scheme v2, which
// covers the whole archive rather than each entry. Any repacking, zipalign
// included, invalidates it. preprocessed tells Soong to install the file
// byte for byte.
//
// dex_preopt off for the same reason: the APK ships optimised and signed, and
// preopting a prebuilt is not needed for it to run.
//
// product partition, not system or vendor. This is bundled software rather
// than part of the platform, and product/app is where that belongs; vendor is
// for hardware support and carries SELinux rules an ordinary app should not be
// subject to.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT INCLUDE. F-Droid installs through the normal package
// installer, so each install and update needs the user to confirm, and the
// user must first allow F-Droid to install unknown apps (it declares
// REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES, which is a user grant rather than something the
// build can pre-approve). Unattended installs would need the separate
// F-Droid Privileged Extension, which has to be a privileged app with its own
// permission allowlist entry. That is deliberately not bundled here: it is a
// larger decision about what gets platform privilege on this device, and the
// store works without it.
//
// It also needs a working network, which on this device means the Wi-Fi or
// wired path being up. There is no offline repository shipped with it.
android_app_import {
    name: "F-Droid",
    apk: "F-Droid.apk",
    presigned: true,
    preprocessed: true,
    dex_preopt: {
        enabled: false,
    },
    product_specific: true,
}
